course: dictionary
lesson: 4083
title: Words that show how bad something is
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how strong a feeling or a thing is. Ghastly is one of them. Read the words around it to find its meaning. In «making uncouth and ghastly noises», the word uncouth means rough and rude, so ghastly is describing noises that are horrible to hear.
explain: Ghastly can describe something scary, like a face, or something simply awful, like weather. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Ghastly is an informal word in British English. People say «ghastly weather» or «a ghastly mistake» to mean something is awful. It is a strong word, so use it when something really is very bad.
fact: Ghastly is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like a ghastly noise or ghastly weather.
fact: Ghastly means very bad, horrible or unpleasant.
fact: Ghastly can mean shocking and frightening, like something terrible to see.
fact: Ghastly can also mean very pale and ill-looking, like a ghost.
fact: The comparative and superlative forms are ghastlier and ghastliest.
exercise: What does ghastly mean in «making uncouth and ghastly noises»? | Quiet and gentle | Sleepy and slow | *Very horrible or unpleasant | Very happy and loud
exercise: Maya says «ghastly weather» means the weather is lovely. What does ghastly mean here? | *Awful and unpleasant | Shiny and bright | Windy and calm | Lovely and warm
exercise: In «making uncouth and ghastly noises», which word tells you the noises were rough and rude? | noises | making | *uncouth | ghastly
test: Which meaning fits ghastly when someone looks like a ghost? | Quick and clever | Very happy and loud | Warm and cosy | *Very pale and ill-looking
test: Which is the superlative form of ghastly? | ghastlying | *ghastliest | ghastlyest | ghastlier
exam: Read «a ghastly mistake». Which word best matches ghastly here? | *Terrible and awful | Bright and shiny | Clever and kind | Quiet and gentle
